t"},{"text":"லை","lemma":"லை","gloss":"second person singular suffix"}],"gloss":"you are not"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"என்","parts":[{"text":"என்","lemma":"யான்","gloss":"my"}],"gloss":"my"},{"surface":"இடம்","parts":[{"text":"இடம்","lemma":"இடம்","gloss":"place"}],"gloss":"place"},{"surface":"யாது","parts":[{"text":"யாது","lemma":"யாது","gloss":"what"}],"gloss":"what is it?"},{"surface":"மற்று","parts":[{"text":"மற்று","lemma":"மற்று","gloss":"then"}],"gloss":"then"},{"surface":"இசை","parts":[{"text":"இசை","lemma":"இசை","gloss":"fame"}],"gloss":"fame"},{"surface":"வெய்யோயே","parts":[{"text":"வெய்","lemma":"வெய்","gloss":"desire"},{"text":"ஓய்","lemma":"ஓய்","gloss":"second person singular suffix"},{"text":"ஏ","lemma":"ஏ","gloss":"emphasis"}],"gloss":"O you who desire!"}]}],"literal":"Fire shining bright-jewels-adorned beautiful body / even than shadow not-leaving your beloved-wife gave-birth-to / fame-abundant son after-being-born come, saying / me here left-behind loveless-one! / without-thinking you-will-remain not / my place what then, O fame-desiring-one!","translation":"O loveless one who left me behind here, saying, 'Come only after your beloved wife, whose beautiful body is adorned with bright jewels shining like fire and who never leaves your side even like a shadow, gives birth to a son of great fame!' You will not remain there without thinking of me. O you who desire fame, what then is my place beside you?","modern":"நெருப்புப் போல ஒளிரும் ஆபரணங்களை அணிந்த அழகிய உடம்பைக் கொண்டவளும், நிழலைப் போல உன்னை விட்டுப் பிரியாதவளுமாகிய உன் மனைவி, புகழ் மிக்க ஒரு மகனைப் பெற்றெடுத்த பிறகு என்னிடம் வா என்று கூறி என்னை இங்கே தவிக்கவிட்டுச் சென்ற அன்பில்லாதவனே! நீ என்னை நினைக்காமல் இருக்க மாட்டாய். புகழை விரும்பும் அரசனே, உன்னருகில் எனக்கான இடம் எது?","explanation":"This poem belongs to the Potuviyal tiṇai and Kaiyaṟunilai tuṟai, written by the poet Pottiyār on the death of his close friend, King Kōpperuñcōḻaṉ. When the king decided to fast unto death (vadakkiruttal), Pottiyār wanted to join him immediately, but the king refused and asked him to return only after his pregnant wife gave birth to a son. Now, after the birth of his son, Pottiyār returns to the spot where the king died and laments his loss. He addresses the deceased king as a 'loveless one' (அன்பிலாள) for leaving him behind, but asserts that the king's soul would still be waiting for him. The poem beautifully captures the deep bond of friendship that transcends death, and the poet's eagerness to join his friend in the afterlife. Key words include 'அன்பிலாள' (loveless one) and 'இசைவெய்யோயே' (O you who desire fame).","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"}}